  "final_text": "All four steps are done, and I checked the dashboard edit and the rule's state by reading each back.\n\n1. **Data view:** \"Kibana Sample Data Flights\" (index `kibana_sample_data_flights`) has 33 fields, including `Carrier`, `Cancelled`, `Dest`, `Origin` and `AvgTicketPrice`. Its time field is `timestamp`.\n2. **Dashboard:** \"S4-539b2357 Flights\" (id `9fee148c-7e5a-4af4-91fb-670c1c212378`) has two panels. One is a line chart of flight count over time, using a date histogram on `timestamp`. The other is a bar chart of flight count by `Carrier`, showing the top 10 carriers.\n3. **Rule:** \"S4-539b2357 Cancelled flights\" (id `ae39c320-32b6-4d67-b1f1-ce59afb6ae59`) is an Elasticsearch query rule. It runs every 1 minute on `kibana_sample_data_flights` and matches `Cancelled: true`. It fires when the count is above 10 in a 5-minute window. It has no actions attached, so it only creates alerts and sends no notifications. Creating it with `enabled: true` worked, so I didn't need to enable it afterwards.\n4. **Edit and read-back:** The carrier panel is now titled \"Flights by carrier\", and the other panel is unchanged. Reading the rule back shows `enabled: true`, and its first run finished with status `ok`.\n\nTIME_FIELD: timestamp\nRULE_ENABLED: yes",
